#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068547 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6cd6c125-ecaf-5847-a802-ec1b3594a15c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is Continuous at a Given Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f12b3891-021d-5ae3-a767-f9d423408ce6" :question/math-academy-id "e-3567" :question/problem "Is the function $y=f(x),$ plotted below, continuous at $x=0?$\n\n![](images/e3/e395863ca66a7e87fa335c56e5b6aeb1ec206893e57b155bf64e5ab07be7545c.png)" :question/worked-solution "Because there is an asymptote at $x=0,$ it is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of $x=0$ without taking our pencil off of the paper.\n\nTherefore, the function $y=f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068548 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/id #uuid "514240a0-8a38-564f-ab4c-d0bacb0c5f6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3568" :question/problem "What are the points of discontinuity of the function $y=g(x),$ plotted below?\n\n![](images/6d/6d0d3c2cd072841546e66d70105436200e316e156901f8fe27dc150e96ff3da4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of discontinuity are at $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and $x=2.5.$ It is impossible to draw the function in the neighborhood of these three points without taking our pencil off of the page."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068549 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "01bca086-0ed4-5626-a357-e0724685f144" :question/math-academy-id "e-3569" :question/problem "![](images/18/1867a80955ae4b456cbcb011eefed96431f2b12926994942613eb947e55e38c6.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=2$\n\n- $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ exists\n\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(2)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. From the graph, we can see that the left and right-sided limits are different:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow \\, 2}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false.  The graph of the function breaks at $x=2.$ It's impossible to draw the graph of $f(x)$ without taking our pencil off of the paper. Therefore, $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=2.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement I is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240787 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f92e521-e7ab-56ba-8eac-0484b140b1a2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Behavior of a Function in the Neighborhood of a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60efdf1c-df12-5856-aa4c-f4f6fc23d42f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437348} {:answer/id #uuid "cea4be79-bc3f-5364-9f0f-d13cc3390e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cea4be79-bc3f-5364-9f0f-d13cc3390e5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437349} :answer-field/id #uuid "67208bb7-2119-51cc-97f2-fc43b1d95b36" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef42aad5-fee8-5449-bbd4-f1ddf4d65e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437350} {:answer/id #uuid "7f3c2501-c170-5ad7-8f83-f02c8744f9ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef42aad5-fee8-5449-bbd4-f1ddf4d65e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437350} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f1b2ad2-b8cf-5da8-ab38-a32e460f880a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e135984-0cd0-5f07-bdfa-2c1791ff56da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437352} {:answer/id #uuid "8ac001f4-ce03-575e-80fe-299ba4a5012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437353}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ac001f4-ce03-575e-80fe-299ba4a5012e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437353} :answer-field/id #uuid "f18fe838-0660-5c6a-8fe2-0675d1705648" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fc9393f2-809c-5769-b329-186102da51c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-104051" :question/problem "![](images/65/65b9b32e8fac6a71141ce820855058367e9235129de7334db35a200905474cc0.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $f(x)$ is defined at $x=5$\n\n- **II.** ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ exists\n\n- **III.** $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=5$" :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $f(5)=2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, from the graph we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,{5}^{+}}f(x) & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,  ${lim}_{x\\to \\,5}f(x)$ doesn't exist\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a jump exactly at $x=5$\nand it's impossible to draw the graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186392602 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "31eacf5a-75a5-5b38-8c24-4b1598ecf3a7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Discontinuity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d495799e-2f9d-5146-a399-506d9d32ad84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c9ba1e63c665ad63597c822828fdf26ec0fefade083df5ea828f57a49d3aa149.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392604} {:answer/id #uuid "c796e42f-5eb7-5d3a-900e-6d5fb58916e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fc/fc1d428500558ed62fbf5f7e0c13604cbc759629cedb63a8f9c2f7fc0b412480.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392605} {:answer/id #uuid "742220de-47f9-5f30-8679-f64626ac489f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d9/d9185fb471c9806d490133c6ea5a54ef69878f89ca0e0be66008ec3626bfe346.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392606} {:answer/id #uuid "11ce7ab1-ca48-5c69-b7f7-b7ff62f3ea3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49e7b3c3cd4e6410b95a307f74c90079ec61c2d3540f86a357db88c77039ca44.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186392607} {:answer/id #uuid "c37ded1b-1a1e-52ed-a977-31d60b87248f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921